# REGRESSION FIXTURE — `g.0 = v` where `g: Guard[(int, int)]`. Track L primary
# (graduated from `known_gaps/scoutE_guard_tuple_field_assign_segv.gg`).
#
# AXIS + CELL (Core #12): TUPLE-FIELD WRITE-THROUGH through a Guard receiver at
# the base cell — element type `int`, index 0, root shape bare local. Sibling
# fixtures cover the axis (index=1, compound-assign, resource-inner, &-root,
# nested, tuple-of-struct, &-field arg, read-side, WriteGuard).
#
# RED-VERIFIED against the pre-fix compiler (tip `1ec3ba55`, in worktree):
#   - C lane:    C-compile ERROR `'Guard__Tuple__int64_t__int64_t' … has no
#                member named 'owner'` at generated `.c` line 3436 — the tuple
#                assign resolver walked to `resolve_tuple_field_type(Guard__…,0)`
#                and stored through the guard wrapper's `owner` slot.
#   - LLVM lane: build OK, run SIGSEGVs (exit 139) — same bogus pointer.
#
# GUARDED MECHANISM: `try_resolve_tuple_field_place` (src/ir/lowering/exprs/mod.rs
# ~3410) now projects through `emit_guard_get_ptr` on a Guard receiver — mirror
# of the struct-face branch at :3239. Revert that block (and the two siblings at
# :2846 and :858) and this fixture RE-REDs. Site (c) at :858 (the READ path)
# also fixed silent-wrong-output on `print(g.0)` — see the read-side fixture.
#
# ⚠ CAVEAT — do NOT collapse the two prints into `print(g.get())`: `print` on a
# `(int, int)` tuple prints garbage (independent pre-existing defect, unrelated
# to this bug). Per-field prints pin the write actually landing.
#
# INTENDED (post-fix): `42\n2` — write to field 0 lands, field 1 untouched.
void main():
    Mutex[(int, int)] m = Mutex[(int, int)]((1, 2))
    Guard[(int, int)] g = m.lock()
    g.0 = 42
    print(g.0)
    print(g.1)
